  "description": "One-minute skill: Taste Preferences: Born or Learned?.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Babies love sweet and hate bitter from their very first taste. Some preferences are hardwired at birth.",
      "visual": "👶"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Sweet preference evolved because sugary foods signal safe calories. Bitter aversion protects against plant toxins.",
      "visual": "🍬"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "But culture overrides biology: children raised on spicy food grow to love it despite innate aversion to capsaicin.",
      "visual": "🌶️"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Supertasters have more taste buds and find bitter foods overwhelming. Non-tasters barely notice bitterness.",
      "visual": "📊"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Your taste preferences are roughly 50% genetic and 50% shaped by experience, exposure, and culture.",
      "visual": "🧬"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "You were born with taste biases. Experience rewrites them throughout your life.",
      "visual": "📝"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Why do humans have an innate preference for sweet tastes?",
    "options": [
      "Sweet signals safe, calorie-rich foods",
      "Sugar is essential for brain development",
      "Sweet receptors mature first"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
